<section
  class="panel"
  id="windows"
  role="tabpanel"
  aria-labelledby="tab-windows"
>
  <p class="muted">
    Windows 10 and 11. Mortar installs for your user, so it needs no
    administrator rights.
  </p>
  <div class="files">
    <a
      class="file primary"
      data-asset="mortar-amd64-installer.exe"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-amd64-installer.exe"
    >
      <strong>Installer, x64</strong><span>Most PCs</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="file"
      data-asset="mortar-arm64-installer.exe"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-arm64-installer.exe"
    >
      <strong>Installer, ARM64</strong
      ><span>Snapdragon and other ARM PCs</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="file"
      data-asset="mortar-windows-amd64.exe"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-windows-amd64.exe"
    >
      <strong>Portable, x64</strong><span>Runs from any folder</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="file"
      data-asset="mortar-windows-arm64.exe"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-windows-arm64.exe"
    >
      <strong>Portable, ARM64</strong><span>Runs from any folder</span>
    </a>
  </div>
  <ol class="steps-list">
    <li>
      Run the installer. It puts Mortar in
      <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Mortar</code> and adds Start Menu and
      Desktop shortcuts.
    </li>
    <li>
      The builds are not code-signed, so SmartScreen says “Windows
      protected your PC” the first time. Choose
      <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>Mortar updates itself from then on.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Scoop</h3>
  <p class="muted">
    From Mortar's own bucket, with every download pinned by its SHA-256. Scoop then handles updates.
  </p>
  <pre><code>scoop bucket add mortar https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/scoop-bucket
scoop install mortar/mortar</code></pre>
</section>

<section
  class="panel"
  id="linux"
  role="tabpanel"
  aria-labelledby="tab-linux"
>
  <div class="tabs modes" role="tablist" aria-label="How to install">
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-linux-store" aria-controls="linux-store">Software center</button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-linux-files" aria-controls="linux-files">Download a file</button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-linux-cli" aria-controls="linux-cli">Command line</button>
  </div>

  <div class="panel sub" id="linux-store" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-linux-store">
    <p class="muted">
      Any distribution with Flatpak, x86_64 or aarch64. Opening the link adds Mortar's signed Flatpak repository in
      GNOME Software, KDE Discover or your system's own app store, which then keeps Mortar up to date. The GNOME
      runtime comes from Flathub.
    </p>
    <div class="files">
      <a class="file primary" href="https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/flatpak/tech.rethunk.Mortar.flatpakref">
        <strong>Install with Flatpak</strong><span>Opens in your software center</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">
      The sandbox only sees the usual game folders. For a game installed elsewhere, run
      <code>flatpak override --user --filesystem=&lt;folder&gt; tech.rethunk.Mortar</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel sub" id="linux-files" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-linux-files">
    <p class="muted">
      The AppImage and the portable program run on Ubuntu 24.04, Debian 13, Fedora 39 or a newer Linux (glibc 2.38)
      and update themselves in place. A single package installs without updates.
    </p>
    <div class="files">
      <a
        class="file primary"
        data-asset="mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage"
      >
        <strong>AppImage, x86_64</strong
        ><span>Nothing else to install</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="file"
        data-asset="mortar-linux-aarch64.AppImage"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-aarch64.AppImage"
      >
        <strong>AppImage, ARM64</strong
        ><span>Nothing else to install</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="file"
        data-asset="mortar-linux-amd64"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-amd64"
      >
        <strong>Portable, x86_64</strong
        ><span>Uses the system's GTK 4 and WebKitGTK 6</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="file"
        data-asset="mortar-linux-arm64"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-arm64"
      >
        <strong>Portable, ARM64</strong
        ><span>Uses the system's GTK 4 and WebKitGTK 6</span>
      </a>
      <a class="file" data-asset="mortar_VERSION_amd64.deb" href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest">
        <strong>.deb, amd64</strong><span>Ubuntu and Debian, no updates</span>
      </a>
      <a class="file" data-asset="mortar-VERSION-1.x86_64.rpm" href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest">
        <strong>.rpm, x86_64</strong><span>Fedora, no updates</span>
      </a>
      <a class="file" data-asset="mortar-VERSION-1-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst" href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest">
        <strong>Arch package, x86_64</strong><span>pacman -U, no updates</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="file"
        data-asset="mortar-linux-x86_64.flatpak"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-x86_64.flatpak"
      >
        <strong>Flatpak bundle, x86_64</strong><span>flatpak install, no updates</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <pre><code>chmod +x mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage
./mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
      The first run adds Mortar to your applications menu and takes
      <code>nxm://</code> links and <code>.mortar</code> files. ARM64 packages are on the
      <a href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest">release page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel sub" id="linux-cli" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-linux-cli">
