    <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-brew" aria-controls="brew"><svg class="mk mk-homebrew" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#homebrew" /></svg>Homebrew</button>
  </div>

  <div
    class="panel sub"
    id="deb"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="tab-deb"
  >
    <p class="muted">
      Ubuntu 24.04, Debian 13 or newer, on amd64 and arm64. Mortar then updates with the rest of your system.
    </p>
    <pre><code>sudo curl -fsSLo /usr/share/keyrings/mortar-archive-keyring.gpg https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/mortar-archive-keyring.gpg
sudo curl -fsSLo /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mortar.list https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/mortar.list
sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt install mortar</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div
    class="panel sub"
    id="fedora"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="tab-fedora"
  >
    <p class="muted">
      Fedora 39 or newer, on x86_64 and aarch64. dnf asks once to import
      the key; check its fingerprint against the one under <a href="#verify">Verify your download</a>.
    </p>
    <pre><code>sudo curl -fsSLo /etc/yum.repos.d/mortar.repo https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/mortar.repo
sudo dnf install mortar</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div
    class="panel sub"
    id="arch"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="tab-arch"
  >
    <p class="muted">
      x86_64 and aarch64. These commands trust the Mortar key, add the
      repository to <code>/etc/pacman.conf</code> and install.
    </p>
    <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/mortar-archive-keyring.asc | sudo pacman-key --add -
sudo pacman-key --lsign-key D8B1C4C205C5FB3633CB8B9900B75959B637DCB5
curl -fsSL https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/pacman.conf | sudo tee -a /etc/pacman.conf
sudo pacman -Sy mortar</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div
    class="panel sub"
    id="flatpak"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="tab-flatpak"
  >
    <p class="muted">
      Any distribution with Flatpak, x86_64 or aarch64. This adds Mortar's signed Flatpak repository, so
      <code>flatpak update</code> keeps it current.
    </p>
    <pre><code>flatpak install --user https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/flatpak/tech.rethunk.Mortar.flatpakref</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="panel sub" id="brew" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-brew">
    <p class="muted">
      From Mortar's own tap, pinned by SHA-256. It installs the AppImage, which needs FUSE 2
      (<code>libfuse2</code>). Homebrew then handles updates.
    </p>
    <pre><code>brew install rethunk-tech/tap/mortar</code></pre>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="deck" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-deck">
  <p class="muted">
    The Steam Deck runs the x86_64 AppImage. Mortar sets up in Desktop Mode, then each profile can be played from
    Game Mode.
  </p>
  <div class="files">
    <a
      class="file primary"
      data-asset="mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage"
    >
      <strong>AppImage, x86_64</strong><span>For the Steam Deck</span>
    </a>
  </div>
  <ol class="steps-list">
    <li>Switch to Desktop Mode and download the AppImage.</li>
    <li>
      Mark it executable: right-click it, <strong>Properties</strong>, <strong>Permissions</strong>, then
      <strong>Is executable</strong>. Run it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Set up your game, then open a profile's menu and choose <strong>Add to Steam</strong>. The profile appears
      in Game Mode, and playing it there starts the game with that profile's mods.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="muted">
    The Flatpak cannot add itself to Steam, so use the AppImage on the Deck. Mortar updates itself in place.
  </p>
</section>

